          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Robert Oppenheimer</strong><br>\nRobert Oppenheimer was born in New York City in 1904. From a young age, he showed an exceptional aptitude for science and learning in general. Educated at the Ethical Culture Society School, his teachers quickly recognized his talents. By the time he was a teenager, he had already mastered several languages and sciences, which paved the way for his future academic successes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Achievements</strong><br>\nOppenheimer pursued higher education at Harvard University, where he studied chemistry. He completed his degree in record time and moved to England to conduct research at the University of Cambridge. Later, he shifted his focus to physics and made significant research contributions, earning a Ph.D. from the University of Göttingen in Germany.</p>\n\n<p><strong>A Theoretical Physicist</strong><br>\nAfter earning his doctorate, Oppenheimer returned to the United States and became a professor at the University of California, Berkeley. He made remarkable strides in theoretical physics, particularly in the areas of quantum mechanics and cosmic ray showers. His work earned him great respect among his peers in the scientific community.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
