            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Oppenheimer was appointed as the scientific director of the Manhattan Project. This secret project was responsible for developing the atomic bomb. He oversaw the construction of the Los Alamos Laboratory in New Mexico, where he coordinated the efforts of the world's leading scientists in the race to build the bomb before Nazi Germany.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ethical Dilemmas</strong><br>\nThe success of the Manhattan Project and the subsequent bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki posed deep ethical questions for Oppenheimer. He experienced a moral conflict over the devastation caused by the atomic bomb, leading him to become a proponent of international control of nuclear power and a campaigner for peaceful uses of atomic energy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>After the War</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Oppenheimer became chairman of the General Advisory Committee of the Atomic Energy Commission. In this role, he advocated for the international control of atomic energy and opposed the development of the hydrogen bomb. His stance put him at odds with many government officials during the Cold War era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
