          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Accusations and Trial</strong><br>\nIn the early 1950s, during a time of heightened anti-communist sentiment, Oppenheimer came under suspicion for his past associations with Communists. In 1954, he faced a security clearance hearing, which resulted in a public airing of his political affiliations and personal associations. Although he defended his loyalty to the United States, his security clearance was revoked.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Reflections</strong><br>\nAfter the trial, Oppenheimer spent the remainder of his career focusing on science and philosophy. He took on a role at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey. Here, he continued to contribute to scientific knowledge and mentored a new generation of physicists, reflecting on the broader implications of science on humanity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nOppenheimer passed away in 1967, leaving behind a complex legacy. He is remembered as the \"father of the atomic bomb,\" a title that acknowledges his scientific brilliance and the weighty historical consequences of his work. His life and career continue to be studied for insights into the ethical responsibilities of scientists.</p>\n"
              },
